Z||000012||fieroshop||04-16-2000||12:58 AM||||Well several people have tested this clutch and have come out by saying that they were able to burn rubber with this clutch but using other name brands they were not able to. The price of the clutch is $375.00 plus shipping. This clutch has been installed in several high power cars. Infact this identical clutch is also fabricated and used in Porsches. So far it holds better then OEM.<BR>I have a picture of this clutch on my web site. It is also on my new parts online catalog. <P>I forgot I also just finished installing this clutch in my GT and I love the way my transmission shifts and how the clutch does not slip.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 04-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||cobra427||04-16-2000||01:41 PM||||Not ever having tried either clutch(F/S or C/W)just by judging by the photos, it would seem to me that the C/W clutch is a little more beefier.Why do I say that? Look at the disc. It has 6 springs as opposed to the 4 which is a stock configuration.They both use kevlar. Both are designed for higher output engines.C/W does not specify as to the clamping force as does the F/S(2400#'s)but I would have to say that it right up there.Give or take a few #'s.I am not inferring that the F/S clutch is a lesser clutch but going in "cold" plus the fact that I am only looking for a good stock upgrade,(besides,starting like a rabbit,makes walking a habit)so with the price factored in,and for reasons stated earlier,I am tilted toward the C/W clutch<BR>Hey, I could be wrong,just my choise.<BR>Hope that no one takes this personal.||||reg||1
Z||000014||RobertVol||04-16-2000||07:57 PM||||I wouldn't Take it that way. I appreciate the opinions. Hmmm I may have to Think about this////// I will be buying one by the end of the month....that gives me some time to think about it ......I have heard ALOT of good things about Chris Wests but If the holdong power of this ither is that good....Hmmmmmmm will have to think ....tough decision ||||reg||1
